## an6.51
an6.51:1.1 | S: Then Venerable Ānanda went up to Venerable Sāriputta, and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then Ven. Ānanda went to Ven. Sāriputta and, on arrival, exchanged courteous greetings with him.
an6.51:1.2 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, Ānanda sat down to one side, and said to Sāriputta: | T: After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, he sat to one side. As he was sitting there he said to Ven. Sāriputta,
an6.51:2.1 | S: “Reverend Sāriputta, how does a mendicant get to hear a teaching they haven’t heard before? How do they remember those teachings they have heard? How do they keep exercising the teachings with which they are already familiar? And how do they come to understand what they haven’t understood before?” | T: “Friend Sāriputta, to what extent does a monk hear Dhamma that he has not heard, do the Dhammas he has heard not get confused, do the Dhammas he has touched with his awareness stay current, and does he understand what (previously) was not understood?”
an6.51:2.2 | S: “Well, Venerable Ānanda, you’re very learned. | T: “Friend Ānanda is learned.
an6.51:2.3 | S: Why don’t you clarify this yourself?” | T: Let the answer occur to him.”
an6.51:2.4 | S: “Well then, Reverend Sāriputta, listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” | T: “In that case, friend Sāriputta, listen to the Dhamma. Pay close attention. I will speak.”
an6.51:2.5 | S: “Yes, reverend,” Sāriputta replied. | T: “As you say, friend,” Ven. Sāriputta responded to him.
an6.51:2.6 | S: Ānanda said this: | T: Ven. Ānanda said,
an6.51:3.1 | S: “Reverend Sāriputta, take a mendicant who memorizes the teaching— | T: “There is the case, friend, where a monk masters the Dhamma:
an6.51:3.2 | S: statements, mixed prose & verse, discussions, verses, inspired exclamations, legends, stories of past lives, amazing stories, and elaborations. | T: dialogues, narratives of mixed prose & verse, explanations, verses, spontaneous exclamations, quotations, birth stories, amazing events, question & answer sessions.
an6.51:3.3 | S: Then, just as they learned and memorized it, they teach others in detail, make them recite in detail, they rehearse it themselves in detail, and they think about and consider the teaching in their heart, examining it with the mind. | T: He teaches the Dhamma in detail—as he has heard it, as he has remembered it—to others. He gets others to recite the Dhamma in detail—as they have heard it, as they have remembered it. He holds a group chanting of the Dhamma in detail—as he has heard it, as he has remembered it. He thinks about & evaluates the Dhamma as he has heard it, as he has remembered it; he contemplates it with his intellect.
an6.51:3.4 | S: They enter the rains retreat in a monastery with senior mendicants who are very learned, inheritors of the heritage, who have memorized the teachings, the monastic law, and the outlines. | T: He enters the Rains in monasteries in which there are senior monks who are learned, who know the tradition, who are holders of the Dhamma, the Vinaya, & the Mātikā.
an6.51:3.5 | S: From time to time they go up to those mendicants and ask them questions: | T: Having approached them periodically, he questions them & quizzes them:
an6.51:3.6 | S: ‘Why, sir, does it say this? What does that mean?’ | T: ‘How is this, venerable sirs? What is the meaning of this?’
an6.51:3.7 | S: Those venerables reveal what is hidden, clarify what is unclear, and dispel doubt regarding the many doubtful matters. | T: They make open for him what wasn’t open, make plain what wasn’t plain, dispel doubt on various doubtful points.
an6.51:3.8 | S: This is how a mendicant gets to hear a teaching they haven’t heard before. It’s how they remember those teachings they have heard. It’s how they keep exercising the teachings with which they are already familiar. And it’s how they come to understand what they haven’t understood before.” | T: “It’s to this extent, friend Sāriputta, that a monk hears Dhamma he has not heard, that the Dhammas he has heard do not get confused, that the Dhammas he has touched with his awareness stay current, and that he understands what (previously) was not understood.”
an6.51:4.1 | S: “It’s incredible, reverend, it’s amazing! How well said this was by Venerable Ānanda! | T: “It’s amazing, my friend. It’s astounding, my friend, how well-said that was by friend Ānanda.
an6.51:4.2 | S: And we will remember Venerable Ānanda as someone who possesses these six qualities. | T: And we will remember friend Ānanda as endowed with these six qualities:
an6.51:4.3 | S: For Ānanda memorizes the teaching … | T: Friend Ānanda has mastered the Dhamma:
an6.51:4.4 | S: statements, mixed prose & verse, discussions, verses, inspired exclamations, legends, stories of past lives, amazing stories, and elaborations. | T: dialogues, narratives of mixed prose and verse, explanations, verses, spontaneous exclamations, quotations, birth stories, amazing events, question & answer sessions.
an6.51:4.5 | P: Āyasmā ānando yathāsutaṁ yathāpariyattaṁ dhammaṁ vitthārena paresaṁ deseti, āyasmā ānando yathāsutaṁ yathāpariyattaṁ dhammaṁ vitthārena paresaṁ vāceti, āyasmā ānando yathāsutaṁ yathāpariyattaṁ dhammaṁ vitthārena sajjhāyaṁ karoti, āyasmā ānando yathāsutaṁ yathāpariyattaṁ dhammaṁ cetasā anuvitakketi anuvicāreti manasānupekkhati. | T: Friend Ānanda teaches the Dhamma in detail—as he has heard it, as he has remembered it—to others. Friend Ānanda gets others to recite the Dhamma in detail—as they have heard it, as they have remembered it. Friend Ānanda holds a group chanting of the Dhamma in detail—as he has heard it, as he has remembered it. Friend Ānanda thinks about & evaluates the Dhamma as he has heard it, as he has remembered it; he contemplates it with his intellect.
an6.51:4.6 | P: Āyasmā ānando yasmiṁ āvāse therā bhikkhū viharanti bahussutā āgatāgamā dhammadharā vinayadharā mātikādharā tasmiṁ āvāse vassaṁ upeti. | T: Friend Ānanda enters the Rains in monasteries in which there are senior monks who are learned, who know the tradition, who are holders of the Dhamma, the Vinaya, & the Mātikā.
an6.51:4.7 | P: Te āyasmā ānando kālena kālaṁ upasaṅkamitvā paripucchati paripañhati: | T: Having approached them periodically, he questions them & quizzes them:
an6.51:4.8 | P: ‘idaṁ, bhante, kathaṁ; imassa kvattho’ti? | T: ‘How is this, venerable sirs? What is the meaning of this?’
an6.51:4.9 | S: Those venerables reveal to Ānanda what is hidden, clarify what is unclear, and dispel doubt regarding the many doubtful matters.” | T: They make open for friend Ānanda what wasn’t open, make plain what wasn’t plain, dispel doubt on various doubtful points.”
an6.51:4.10 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an6.61
an6.61:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
an6.61:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Varanasi in the deer park at Isipatana. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Vārāṇasī at the Deer Park at Isipatana.
an6.61:1.3 | S: Now at that time, after the meal, on return from almsround, several senior mendicants sat together in the pavilion and this discussion came up among them, | T: And on that occasion a large number of elder monks, after the meal, on returning from their alms round, were sitting gathered together in the assembly hall when this discussion arose:
an6.61:1.4 | S: “Reverends, this was said by the Buddha in ‘The Way to the Far Shore’, in ‘The Questions of Metteyya’: | T: “It was said by the Blessed One in The Way to the Further Shore, in Metteyya’s Question [Snp5.3]:
an6.61:2.1 | S: ‘The thoughtful one, knowing both ends, | T: ‘Whoever, a thinker, knowing both sides,
an6.61:2.2 | S: is not stuck in the middle. | T: doesn’t adhere in between:
an6.61:2.3 | S: He is a great man, I declare; | T: He I call a great person.
an6.61:2.4 | S: he has escaped the seamstress here.’ | T: He here has gone past the seamstress.’
an6.61:3.1 | S: But what is one end? What’s the second end? What’s the middle? And who is the seamstress?” | T: “Which, friends, is the first side? Which is the second side? What is in-between? Who is the seamstress?”
an6.61:3.2 | S: When this was said, one of the mendicants said to the senior mendicants: | T: When this was said, a monk said to the elder monks,
an6.61:3.3 | S: “Contact, reverends, is one end. The origin of contact is the second end. The cessation of contact is the middle. And craving is the seamstress, | T: “Contact, friends, is the first side, the origination of contact the second side, and the cessation of contact is in between. Craving is the seamstress—
an6.61:3.4 | S: for craving weaves one to being regenerated in one state of existence or another. | T: for craving stitches one to the production of this or that very becoming.
an6.61:3.5 | S: That’s how a mendicant directly knows what should be directly known and completely understands what should be completely understood. Knowing and understanding thus they make an end of suffering in this very life.” | T: It’s to this extent, friends, that a monk directly knows what should be directly known, comprehends what should be comprehended. Directly knowing what should be directly known, comprehending what should be comprehended, he is one who puts an end to suffering & stress in the here & now.”
an6.61:4.1 | S: When this was said, one of the mendicants said to the senior mendicants: | T: When this was said, another monk said to the elder monks,
an6.61:4.2 | S: “The past, reverends, is one end. The future is the second end. The present is the middle. And craving is the seamstress … | T: “The past, friends, is the first side, the future the second side, and the present is in between. Craving is the seamstress—
an6.61:4.3 | P: taṇhā hi naṁ sibbati tassa tasseva bhavassa abhinibbattiyā. | T: for craving stitches one to the production of this or that very becoming.
an6.61:4.4 | S: That’s how a mendicant directly knows … an end of suffering in this very life.” | T: It’s to this extent, friends, that a monk… is one who puts an end to suffering & stress in the here & now.”
an6.61:5.1 | S: When this was said, one of the mendicants said to the senior mendicants: | T: When this was said, another monk said to the elder monks,
an6.61:5.2 | S: “Pleasant feeling, reverends, is one end. Painful feeling is the second end. Neutral feeling is the middle. And craving is the seamstress … | T: “Pleasant feeling, friends, is the first side, painful feeling the second side, and neither-pleasant-nor-painful feeling is in between. Craving is the seamstress—
an6.61:5.3 | P: taṇhā hi naṁ sibbati tassa tasseva bhavassa abhinibbattiyā. | T: for craving stitches one to the production of this or that very becoming.
an6.61:5.4 | S: That’s how a mendicant directly knows … an end of suffering in this very life.” | T: It’s to this extent, friends, that a monk… is one who puts an end to suffering & stress in the here & now.”
an6.61:6.1 | S: When this was said, one of the mendicants said to the senior mendicants: | T: When this was said, another monk said to the elder monks,
an6.61:6.2 | S: “Name, reverends, is one end. Form is the second end. Consciousness is the middle. And craving is the seamstress … | T: “Name, friends, is the first side, form the second side, and consciousness is in between. Craving is the seamstress—
an6.61:6.3 | P: taṇhā hi naṁ sibbati tassa tasseva bhavassa abhinibbattiyā. | T: for craving stitches one to the production of this or that very becoming.
an6.61:6.4 | S: That’s how a mendicant directly knows … an end of suffering in this very life.” | T: It’s to this extent, friends, that a monk… is one who puts an end to suffering & stress in the here & now.”
an6.61:7.1 | S: When this was said, one of the mendicants said to the senior mendicants: | T: When this was said, another monk said to the elder monks,
an6.61:7.2 | S: “The six interior sense fields, reverends, are one end. The six exterior sense fields are the second end. Consciousness is the middle. And craving is the seamstress … | T: “The six internal sense-media, friends, are the first side, the six external sense-media the second side, and consciousness is in between. Craving is the seamstress—
an6.61:7.3 | P: taṇhā hi naṁ sibbati tassa tasseva bhavassa abhinibbattiyā. | T: for craving stitches one to the production of this or that very becoming.
an6.61:7.4 | S: That’s how a mendicant directly knows … an end of suffering in this very life.” | T: It’s to this extent, friends, that a monk… is one who puts an end to suffering & stress in the here & now.”
an6.61:8.1 | S: When this was said, one of the mendicants said to the senior mendicants: | T: When this was said, another monk said to the elder monks,
an6.61:8.2 | S: “Substantial reality, reverends, is one end. The origin of substantial reality is the second end. The cessation of substantial reality is the middle. And craving is the seamstress, | T: “Self-identification, friends, is the first side, the origination of self-identification the second side, and the cessation of self-identification is in between. Craving is the seamstress—
an6.61:8.3 | S: for craving weaves one to being regenerated in one state of existence or another. | T: for craving stitches one to the production of this or that very becoming.
an6.61:8.4 | S: That’s how a mendicant directly knows what should be directly known and completely understands what should be completely understood. Knowing and understanding thus they make an end of suffering in this very life.” | T: It’s to this extent, friends, that a monk directly knows what should be directly known, comprehends what should be comprehended. Directly knowing what should be directly known, comprehending what should be comprehended, he is one who puts an end to suffering & stress in the here & now.”
an6.61:9.1 | S: When this was said, one of the mendicants said to the senior mendicants: | T: “When this was said, one of the monks said to the elder monks,
